readings.c2s3:
''balcho'', a scaffold, = E. ''balk'', a beam, etc.: see ''balk''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] '''1.''' A stage or platform projecting from the wall of a building within or without, supported by columns, pillars, or consoles, and encompassed with a balustrade, railing, or parapet. Outer balconies are common before windows, and inner ones in ball-rooms, public halls, etc.
{{smaller block|The flourish of trumpets and kettledrums from a high ''balcony'', which overlooked the hall, announced the entrance of the maskers.
{{right|''Scott'', Kenilworth, II. xviii.}}}}
'''2.''' In theaters, a gallery occupying various positions. {{smaller|In some theaters it is a raised tier of seats surrounding the parquette; in others it takes the place of the dress-circle; and in others still it is the gallery immediately behind or above the dress-circle.}}

'''bald'''<sup>1</sup> (bâld), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''balde'', ''baulde'', ''bal’d'', ''ball’d'', < ME. ''balde'', ''belde'', earlier ''balled'', ''ballid'', ''ballede'', bald; of uncertain origin, (1) by some regarded as identical with the rare early ME. ''ballede'', in the apparent sense of rotund, corpulent, applied to the body, lit. ‘balled,’ round like a ball (< ''ball''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>), and hence, perhaps, of the head, smooth, hairless; otherwise (2) perhaps < ''ball'', a white streak or spot (a word of Celtic origin not found in ME., but prob. then existent: see ''ball''<sup>3</sup>), + ''-ede'', an adj. suffix connected with ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Wanting hair, as the head, in some part (usually the top, or front and top) where it naturally grows; partly or wholly deprived of hair on the head, as a person.
{{smaller block|His heed was ''ballid'' and schon as eny glas.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 198.}}}}

readings.c2s4:
¶
{{smaller block|Cæsar, . . . because his head was ''bald'', covered that defect with laurels.
{{right|''Addison.''}}}}
'''2.''' Without the natural or usual covering of the head or top; bareheaded: as, a ''bald'' oak; a ''bald'' mountain.
{{smaller block|No question asked him by any of the senators, but they stand ''bald'' before him.
{{right|''Shak.'', Cor., iv. 5.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Thy ''bald'', awful head, O sovran Blanc!</poem>
{{right|''Coleridge'', Chamouni.}}}}
'''3.''' Destitute of beard or awn: as, ''bald'' wheat. —'''4.''' Wanting force or meaning; meager; paltry: as, a ''bald'' sermon; a ''bald'' truism.—'''5.''' Destitute of appropriate ornament; too bare, plain, or literal; unadorned; inelegant: as, “a ''bald'' translation,” ''Longfellow'', Hyperion, iii. 6.

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|He [Milton] could stoop to a plain style, sometimes even to a ''bald'' style; but false brilliancy was his utter aversion.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Milton.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ghastly thro’ the drizzling rain
On the ''bald'' street breaks the blank day.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam, vii.}}}}
'''6.''' Bare; open; undisguised.
{{smaller block|A ''bald'' egotism which is quite above and beyond selfishness.
{{right|''Lowell'', Among my Books, 1st ser., p. 314.}}}}
'''7.''' Having white on the face or head: specifically applied to several birds: as, the ''bald'' buzzard, eagle, etc.

'''II.''' ''n.'' A natural meadow or grassy plain occurring on the rounded summit of a high mountain: a term in use in the southern extension of the Appalachian ranges, where a number of the highest knobs have their dome-shaped tops entirely bare of trees.

'''bald'''<sup>1</sup>† (bâld), ''v. t.'' [< ''bald''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.''] To make bald; deprive of hair.

'''bald'''<sup>2</sup>†, ''a.'' An obsolete and dialectal form of ''bold''. {{smaller|It is retained in this spelling as an element in certain proper names of Anglo-Saxon or Old High German origin: as, ''Baldwin'', ''Archibald'', ''Ethelbald'', etc.}}

'''baldachin''' (bal′da-kin), ''n.'' [In def. 1 also formerly ''baldakin'', ''bäldekin'', and earlier ''baudekin'', q. v.; in def. 2 also ''baldaquin'', and, as It. or Sp., ''baldacchino'', ''baldaquino''; < F. ''baldaquin'' = Sp. ''baldaquino'' = Pg. ''baldaquim'', < It. ''baldacchino'' (ML. ''baldakinus'', etc.), a canopy, < ''Baldacco'', It. form of ''Bagdad'' (Ar. ''Baghdad''), where a rich cloth used for such canopies was manufactured.] '''1†.''' Same as ''baudekin''.—'''2.''' A canopy of various kinds. {{smaller|(''a'') A portable decorative covering, borne in ceremonial processions as a sign of rank}}

